// Amendments & Placeholders log — parsed verbatim from the backlog workbook.
window.AMENDMENTS = {"A5": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Covert observation clarification", "full": "A5. Covert Observation \u2014 Clarification of Meaning\n\nStories affected: CMS-100 (Epic) Covert Assessment Recording \u00b7 CMS-101 (Epic) Covert Assessment Analytics \u00b7 CMS-F-US-047 (Func) Add Covert observation\nChange type: Clarify acceptance criteria\nSource: Security Day 1 \u2014 covert observation clarification\n\nWhat to change: Clarify throughout all affected stories: \"Covert\" means \"unscheduled/unannounced\" \u2014 the individual does not know in advance when it will occur. \"Covert\" does NOT mean \"hidden from record\" \u2014 once completed, it IS visible on the individual's competency record and to their manager. Remove any AC language implying the observation is concealed from the individual's record after completion. Retain the concept that covert observations are not pre-scheduled.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A25": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Mirror at stage/level (confirmed in wireframe)", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-119  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-108\u2013US-113      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2; post-workshop wireframe check      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A25]\n\n**Change.  **No story change required. A post-workshop check of the v0.5 wireframe confirms the mirror function already creates a mirrored relationship between a competency stage or level and other competency tracks, not only between whole competencies. The question raised in the workshop is therefore closed: editing a mirrored source stage propagates to every instance behind the existing impact-assessment gate, as needed.\n\n**Why.  **Engineering's \u201ccore\u201d training band (13 courses) is common to ~350 technician records held across 15 master spreadsheets; mirroring at stage level is what removes that re-keying. The capability is already present, so the stories and the v1.3 dictionary are aligned \u2014 no amendment is needed.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A26": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Scheduled / pre-published status", "full": "**Artifact  **[Dictionary]  (mechanism already in [Epic] CMS-9, CMS-112)      **Source  **Day 1 Session 1; post-workshop check      **Cluster  **C0 Foundations      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A26]\n\n**Change.  **The publication mechanism \u2014 a future-dated version that supersedes the live one automatically on its active-from date \u2014 is already present in the v0.5 stories. The change is in the dictionary: add a lifecycle status between draft and published for a version that is complete and scheduled but not yet live (\u201cscheduled\u201d / \u201cpre-published\u201d), with its active-from date visible, so the status set is no longer only draft / published / archived.\n\n**Why.  **Divisions know in advance when a standard changes (a liquid-rules change in Security was the example). The mechanism exists; what was missing is a status that distinguishes a scheduled, not-yet-live version from a draft.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A27": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Toolbox Talk formal", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-110  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 1; Day 1 Session 2 (Engineering app)      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A27]\n\n**Change.  **Reclassify Toolbox Talk as a formal short safety briefing recorded against competence to maintain, capturing who delivered it and who was present. Retain the term \u201cToolbox Talk\u201d across all divisions rather than renaming to \u201cbriefing\u201d.\n\n**Why.  **The squad were clear the Toolbox Talk is a tracked, attributable event (Engineering already runs it through a read-and-understand app), not an informal chat. The term is understood industry-wide, so consistency is better served by keeping it.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A28": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Bulletin acknowledge-not-accept", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-34, CMS-108, SYS-10  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 1      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A28]\n\n**Change.  **Hold the bulletin language precisely to acknowledge (read-and-understand), never accept \u2014 a recipient may not agree with the content but must acknowledge it. Confirm a bulletin can carry the read-and-understand function for a Toolbox Talk, and can be chained to a follow-on confidence self-assessment (see A29).\n\n**Why.  **The acknowledge/accept distinction was settled in an earlier validation round and must not regress. Linking bulletin \u2192 Toolbox Talk \u2192 confidence check is how the system encourages the right manager conversation when someone is not yet confident.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A29": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Confidence self-assessment", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-34  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 1      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A29]\n\n**Change.  **Provide a confidence self-assessment that can follow a Toolbox Talk or bulletin: the individual records whether they are confident to perform the relevant duty. A \u201cnot confident\u201d response holds them off that duty and surfaces a manager one-to-one rather than silently passing.\n\n**Why.  **This systematises good manager behaviour \u2014 most will answer \u201cconfident\u201d, but the one who does not is caught before deployment rather than after an incident.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A30": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Digital assessment, no transcription", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-100, CMS-101  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-047      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Security AS-IS      **Cluster  **C1 Assessment Content Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A30]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm that auto-marked multiple-choice assessments produce the result and the evidence digitally, with no trainer re-keying. The system's timestamped record of who sat the assessment, when and with what outcome is the audit evidence; there is no print-mark-scan step.\n\n**Why.  **Security today runs written assessments through Microsoft Forms, then a trainer hand-writes the result onto paper, which is scanned into the CTMD store \u2014 three manual touches and a known error source. ASM short-answer marking remains a human step and is not in scope here.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A31": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "ASM certificate production", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS (certificate production story)      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Security AS-IS      **Cluster  **C4b Assessment Types      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A31]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm the certificate-production capability survives: the v0.5 \u201cdownload certificate\u201d story appears to have been replaced by another story, so verify it is still in scope. Tighten it for the one confirmed live use case: the Aviation Security Manager (ASM) certificate, which must carry the trainer name and SIN, an approved layout and the company number. The need is an admin ability to produce a digitally watermarked certificate as authentic proof generated from a record \u2014 not a manually produced Word / PowerPoint document anyone could replicate. Confirm whether it is a Must or a Could for MVP.\n\n(Potentially an expansion of CMS-24 - SSOW Digital Appointment Letters to produce a certificate and potentially CMS-36 Competency Certificate Download) - but there may be other/better ones)\n\n**Why.  **ASM is the only place a certificate is generated today (in Word / PowerPoint, manually), which carries no authority of record. A system-generated, watermarked certificate gives provenance; pinning the story to that single use case avoids over-building certificate generation no other division needs.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A32": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Trainer track / SIN", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-1 (A13b), CMS-12 (A13b), CMS-102      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Security trainers      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A32]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm a trainer competency track (GSO + GSS + L380 + RFX + ASM, plus CPD and annual recurrents) whose maintenance keeps the system privilege to invigilate and assess. The CAA-issued SIN (Certified Instructor Number) is captured as an admin-only, free-text evidence requirement; it lives in the CAA system, the CMS holds the reference.\n\n**Why.  **Couples the privilege to assess to demonstrably-current trainer competence (the privilege\u2013competence link), which answers the auditor's \u201chow do you assure the assessor?\u201d question.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A33": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "End-of-course report \u2192 system", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-30  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Security      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A33]\n\n**Change.  **Replace the manually-compiled end-of-course report with system-generated reporting plus a routed notification and a team dashboard. Outcomes (pass / fail / NA, restrictions, currency, demotions) route to colleague scheduling, learning ops, training managers and the Security Operations Manager (SOM); the SOM checks a team dashboard rather than relying on an email being read in time.\n\n**Why.  **The current report is hand-typed and emailed to several recipients; its weakness is the timing gap (see P-007). Surfacing status on a dashboard the SOM habitually checks closes part of that gap without a downstream rostering integration.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A34": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Two observation types / coaching lifts", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-20, CMS-111  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-073\u2013US-082      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Security manager-referral workflow      **Cluster  **C3 Competency Lifecycle      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A34]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm two observation roles in the lifecycle: an assessor/observer input that can trigger a restriction or competence revocation, and a coaching observation that lifts it. A restriction conditioned on coaching cannot auto-lift until the coaching activity is logged.\n\n**Why.  **Mirrors the Security manager-referral path: a failed covert / Redline / low-TIP outcome restricts a duty, a manager referral books one-to-one coaching, and only the logged coaching outcome clears the restriction.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A35": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Dual-party observation form", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] AS-58, AS-92, AS-126, CMS-112  \u00b7  [Functional] AS-F A-021, A-022, A-077\u2013A-079      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Airside AirDat      **Cluster  **C4b Assessment Types      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A35]\n\n**Change.  **Extend the observation form configuration to enable an observation to have features that allows the assessor to complete an observation and then a separate delegate attestation is then unlocked for the assessee only \u2014 it cannot be started until the observation is logged, and it is timestamped against the assessee's own login. In a shared room (Security) this is a digital signature via single sign-on, not a wet signature.\n\n**Why.  **AirDat's two-party sign-off proves the candidate was present; the current architecture records observation answers but not who attested. Gating the attestation behind the logged observation makes the audit trail tamper-evident.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A36": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Form version tracking", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-112  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Airside      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A36]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm that observation forms, checklists, exams and question banks are versioned objects and that the version in force at the time of an observation is recorded and visible. An author editing a form should see that it is creating a new version.\n\n**Why.  **On AirDat today a one-word edit to an assessment form takes effect silently with no indication the form changed \u2014 the assessor cannot tell which version they are using.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A37": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Configurable result format / 0\u20135 scale", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] AS-58  \u00b7  [Functional]  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Airside      **Cluster  **C4b Assessment Types      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A37]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm the result format of an assessment is configurable \u2014 pass/fail (binary), a level scale, or a percentage pass mark. Where a level scale is used, the level selection is itself a question type on the observation form, and commentary can be made mandatory. Record Airside's scale move from 1\u20135 to 0\u20135 (0\u20133 fail, 4 meets the minimum standard, 5 exceeds), with criteria strengthened to reduce subjectivity.\n\n**Why.  **Airside is rewriting its competency assessments with a wider scale and firmer criteria; Engineering's grading scale is a separate open confirmation (see P-003 context).", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A38": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Equivalency patterns", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-118  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2; Day 2 Session 3      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A38]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm equivalency supports two patterns the operation relies on: a competency observation in life is equivalent to refresher training (so completing either maintains competence \u2014 Airside firearms example), and a single assessment event completed for one reason can satisfy a separate requirement window (a DNXCT done after a TIP failure can count toward a recurrent, if configured).\n\n**Why.  **Lets the operation avoid double-running an assessment within its window, while keeping a clean record of why each event happened.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A39": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Configurable report builder", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] SYS-70  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 1 Session 2 \u2014 Airside transport reporting      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A39]\n\n**Change.  **Specify that reports are built as rows = people \u00d7 columns = requirement, filtered by audience (role, department, watch), so a division can reproduce its current matrices and add a new requirement (for example, a 51st vehicle) without a developer. Pair with a hybrid search-and-dashboard view (\u201cshow everyone competent on X\u201d).\n\n**Why.  **Airside's transport reporting (\u224850 vehicles, \u224855 people) and the engineering trackers are all variants of this one shape; building it as configuration rather than hard-coded reports avoids a coding change every time the operation changes.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A40": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Widgetised dashboards", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] SYS-107      **Source  **Day 2 Session 3 \u2014 challenge #5      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A40]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm dashboards and reports are widgetised and user-configurable rather than hard-coded: a user can assemble a dashboard, save it as a template, and push a template out. No fixed per-division dashboard set is specified up front.\n\n**Why.  **Emulating every Heathrow reporting permutation up front is intractable; a configurable widget architecture is lower risk provided the data points exist (which the scheme-manual work secures).", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A41": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Rich-text descriptions", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-4  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 1      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A41]\n\n**Change.  **Specify that competency and requirement description fields support rich text (bold, italics, bullets), not plain text, so descriptions render with fidelity in the system and in any exported scheme document.\n\n**Why.  **Authors will naturally format descriptions; a plain-text field cannot reproduce that on export and undermines the scheme-manual-from-system goal (B2).", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A42": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Pass-by-attendance toggle", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-4, CMS-9  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 3 \u2014 challenge #6      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A42]\n\n**Change.  **Add a per-requirement toggle so completion can be recognised by attendance (a trainer marks attended / non-attended) as well as by assessment outcome. This is the interim manual route for e-learning completion while the Oracle LMS integration is unresolved (see P-009).\n\n**Why.  **Until the LMS feed exists, a trainer attesting attendance is the controllable way to mark a mandatory module complete against a competence.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A43": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Role-tag track assignment", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-1, CMS-12  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 1      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A43]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm competency-track assignment is driven by role mapping: a role maps to a fixed stack of competencies and is applied automatically (Security), with a template-plus-manager hybrid for Engineering. Where Theo does not hold the needed role delineation (for example a \u201cPlus Team Leader Specialist\u201d that Theo records only as \u201ctechnician\u201d), a manual assignment option applies.\n\n**Why.  **Takes the administrative burden out of assignment wherever the role data supports it, while not blocking on a parallel Theo data-cleanup track.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A44": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Self-registration dual-track", "full": "**Artifact  **[Functional] NEW-F-049      **Source  **Day 2 Session 2      **Cluster  **C3 Competency Lifecycle      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A44]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm the v0.5 self-registration story: an individual can register interest in a higher competence (Engineering), running a dual track \u2014 the competencies required to do their current job, plus the aspirational ones they have chosen to pursue. Registration informs the manager but does not require permission and does not, of itself, confer pay progression.\n\n**Why.  **Resolves the union tension that people should not be blocked from aspiring upward, while aspiration alone does not trigger reward.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A45": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Offline exam fallback / timed", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] NEW epic (offline exam fallback)  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 2      **Cluster  **C4a Assessment Delivery      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A45]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm the offline / paper-based exam fallback: print an exam marking sheet and enter the result manually, retaining a full digital audit trail (the scan becomes a digital record, not a filing-cabinet entry). Confirm timed and untimed delivery variants exist as reasonable adjustments.\n\n**Why.  **Reasonable adjustment and union fairness require that any exam can, if necessary, be delivered on paper without losing the digital record \u2014 and that timed delivery is available where the syllabus requires it.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A46": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Absence / pause / RTD", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-117  \u00b7  [Functional] NEW-F-051, NEW-F-054  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 3 \u2014 challenge #8      **Cluster  **C3 Competency Lifecycle      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A46]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm the absence handling already added in v0.5: a privileged user (manager / administrator) records an absence (start, expected return, type); competency requirements are paused with no expiry countdown running; on return the system calculates the absence duration and assigns the appropriate return-to-duty track, configurable per division, with the individual shown as restricted until that track completes. An automatic absence feed from Theo / HRMS is a later aspiration, not MVP. Per the clarification round (CMS-117), for Security the return-to-duty must also recognise medical restrictions applied not by Learning but by Customer Services / Occupational Health; the transaction references the medical-competency handling (CMS-21) and the restriction framework (CMS-111) so a medically-driven restriction on return is captured. The equivalent Airside / Engineering return-to-duty handling is an open action (Sheena).\n\n**Why.  **Neither the CMS nor Theo knows who is absent without being told; a human-entered absence is the interim, with the system fields and logic built so the feed can be added later. The term for the paused state is unresolved (see P-002).", "status": "Pending confirmation"}, "A47": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "TIP outcome-driven feed", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-100  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-047      **Source  **Day 2 Session 3 \u2014 challenge #7      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A47]\n\n**Change.  **Refine the TIP stories so the system consumes a TIP feed as an activity with a date range, a person and an outcome, and applies a configured rule (outcome \u2192 coaching requirement / full-retrain requirement / restrict-or-revoke). The system does not attempt to compute the bracket or the repeat-offender logic \u2014 the Security analyst sets the outcome; the threshold-impact behaviour is already in v0.5.\n\n**Why.  **Keeps the build simple and durable: a consistent outcome feed drives the automation, and the same import script serves the first (historical) load and the recurring delta.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A48": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Restriction refined / revoke", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-20, CMS-111  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-073\u2013US-082  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 3 \u2014 challenge #2      **Cluster  **C3 Competency Lifecycle      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A48], P-001\n\n**Change.  **Confirm restrictions remain, refined: they are task / duty based, not whole-person, and may be applied automatically (a failed routine requirement revokes competence, which makes the duty undeployable) or consciously by a person (an investigation). Adopt \u201crevoke\u201d for competence loss in place of \u201cdemotion\u201d. Override remains, fully auditable, with a mandatory reason field. The precise per-type changes are blocked pending the restrictions table (see P-001).\n\n**Why.  **Distinguishing automatic competence-revocation from a deliberate restriction gives meaningful reporting (how many are restricted vs not-competent vs exempt) instead of one conflated figure.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A49": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Urgent restriction push", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-111, SYS-10  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 3 \u2014 challenge #2      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7A49], P-007\n\n**Change.  **Confirm that a new restriction can fire a configurable urgent push notification, requiring acknowledgement, to managers / SOMs who hold a device, alongside the team dashboard. This is the behaviour-change mechanism that mitigates the late-notification risk while no downstream rostering integration exists.\n\n**Why.  **A late-night course outcome can otherwise reach colleague scheduling only after the restricted officer has already started the next shift; a persistent push the SOM must acknowledge is harder to miss than an email.\n\n## Clarification round (9 June) \u2014 acknowledged story updates\n\nThese amendments arise from the pre-workshop clarification round of 9 June. The team raised clarification points against the v0.5 stories; the DBLX responses were captured in the workbook and shared back, but the story edits they imply were deliberately not yet actioned. Where a response was a pure clarification, or confirmed existing configurability, or deferred a point to the workshop (the nine challenge areas), no entry is needed. The entries below are the ones where a story change was agreed and remains outstanding. Each cites the workbook story it sits against.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A50": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Notification recipient-routing", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-29, CMS-30, CMS-31, CMS-32, CMS-76; ASS-9, ASS-44  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (CMS-30/31/32; ASS-9/44)      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Capture as explicit functional detail that competency-expiry reminders are configurable by recipient and audience, and are suppressed for populations with no agency over their own rostering (Security Officers, Airside operational). \u201cYou have been booked onto recurrent / RTD on [date]\u201d notifications remain \u2014 they are actionable \u2014 while expiry-countdown reminders to the individual do not fire for these audiences and instead route to Learning Ops / Scheduling. A restriction notification (competence lapsed \u2192 restriction applied) is a distinct, useful trigger handled via the restriction framework (CMS-111), not the expiry path. Assessment-outcome notifications (ASS-44) include any restriction applied, with Scheduling / Learning Ops as a selectable recipient.\n\n**Why.  **The mechanism already exists; the team's point is a real requirement \u2014 constant expiry reminders to officers who cannot act on them add stress without value. This records the routing as deliberate configuration rather than leaving it implicit.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A51": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Configurable results release to assessee", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-35, ASS-58, ASS-125  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (CMS-35)      **Cluster  **C4a Assessment Delivery      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Make the visibility and timing of results to the assessee configurable per assessment (instant / on trainer release / delayed / withheld), distinct from the trainer-side invigilation and release controls. Confirm the result-release rules with Learning Delivery, including externally-delivered technical learning where HAL does not control the result detail.\n\n**Why.  **A Security officer sitting around 19 modules seeing instant results could bias subsequent modules; the per-assessment toggle is exactly what the configuration layer exists to serve.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A52": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Certificate download control + exceptions", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-36, ASS-21  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (CMS-36; ASS-21)      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Confirm the v0.5 amendment removing certificate download / save in favour of the QR competency passport (expiry removed; looked up in-system as the real-time source of truth; available via Apple / Google Wallet). Add that whether any download or certificate access is permitted is a configurable toggle per competence and audience, to support two legitimate exceptions: (a) externally-certificated courses such as IPAP, where a supplier certificate may reasonably be downloadable, and (b) learning-agreement / repayment-clause cases, where access is withheld so a leaver cannot use a certificate externally during a clawback period.\n\n**Why.  **Distinct from certificate production (A31); the concern is access control and authenticity of record, not generation.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A53": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Dashboard shows restrictions + route-to-clear", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-45, CMS-111  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (CMS-45)      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Note on the personal competency dashboard that for a Security Officer it must render active restrictions and, where possible, what is required to remove each one, drawn from the restriction framework (CMS-111).\n\n**Why.  **The individual needs to see not only that they are restricted but the route to clear it.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A54": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Offline mode repositioned + invigilation-gated", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-59; ASS-19, ASS-114, ASS-115, ASS-125  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (CMS-59; ASS-19)      **Cluster  **Varies by artifact: CMS-59 \u2192 C6; ASS-19 \u2192 C6; ASS-114 \u2192 C4a; ASS-115 \u2192 TBC (no register entry); ASS-125 \u2192 C1      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Reposition offline / disaster-recovery mode away from a self-service employee story toward a system capability (aligns with open question LHR014, for IT). For assessments, offline working is gated by the invigilation / integrity configuration (ASS-114/115 lockdown; ASS-125 invigilated sessions) and applies to all colleagues sitting assessments, not freely available. A worked example is a competency check or QR lookup at height or in tunnels with limited signal.\n\n**Why.  **Offline is unlikely to be an end-user action, and an un-invigilated offline assessment would breach Security's integrity requirements.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A55": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "TNCA create/display/track lifecycle", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-51, CMS-69  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (CMS-69)      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR Action SM1]\n\n**Change.  **Adjust the TNCA management story wording and break out the create / display / track lifecycle properly: the T&C team create, display and track Training Needs Competency Analyses in-system, with responsibility for meeting the competency requirement sitting with the owning team. Related provision sits in CMS-51 (TNCA progression). To be co-developed with the Engineering SME rather than specified in isolation.\n\n**Why.  **Adam's point that the wording conflates the analysis tool with requirement ownership; the lifecycle deserves explicit stories, developed in the Engineering deep-dive.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A56": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Audit generalised beyond CAA", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-90; ASS-108, ASS-110, ASS-122, ASS-124  \u00b7  [Functional]  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (CMS-90; ASS-108/110/122/124)      **Cluster  **C0 Foundations      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Generalise the audit-trail and activity-log language so it is regulator-agnostic \u2014 the immutable audit record is critical for all audits and all regulators (CAA, DfT and others), not Security / CAA alone. A broader assurance capability (the Audit Hub) is proposed separately and held at P-012 pending the covering note.\n\n**Why.  **Adam's correction; several stories carried CAA-specific language that under-states the requirement.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A57": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Configurable retake routing", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] ASS-29, ASS-63  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (ASS-29; ASS-63)      **Cluster  **C4a Assessment Delivery      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Make the assessment-retake route configurable by audience, so that for populations who do not self-schedule (Security Officers, Airside, externally-run Engineering courses, FHS) retakes are coordinated by Learning Ops / Scheduling rather than self-requested by the individual.\n\n**Why.  **Mirrors how these audiences are rescheduled today.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A58": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Technical-readiness check repositioned", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] ASS-38  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (ASS-38)      **Cluster  **C4a Assessment Delivery      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Reflect that the pre-assessment technical-readiness check is a Training Delivery team responsibility for device-less audiences (Security Officers), not an individual action.\n\n**Why.  **Security Officers have no personal devices, so the check cannot be a self-service step for them.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A59": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Assessment Administrator role defined", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] ASS-69; SYS role stories  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (ASS-69)      **Cluster  **C0 Foundations      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Add an explicit definition of the Assessment Administrator role \u2014 what it is, how the privilege is allocated and maintained, and who designates a holder \u2014 supporting multiple holders scoped by area (multiple Tech Learning and Engineering colleagues), via the role-based access model.\n\n**Why.  **The team asked who an Assessment Administrator is and how it is kept current; the role needs governance, not just a label.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A60": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Manual external entry stores scanned certs", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] ASS-72, ASS-73  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (ASS-72/73)      **Cluster  **C4b Assessment Types      **Report  **[WR \u00a71], P-004, P-006\n\n**Change.  **Confirm in the acceptance criteria that manual external-assessment entry stores externally-issued scanned certificates, and that the external-assessment verification workflow carries clear step-by-step procedural guidance (a candidate for change-management / training material). FHS competency ownership per role remains open (see P-006).\n\n**Why.  **Closes the third-party evidence loop while keeping externals out of the HAL environment (see P-004).", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A61": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Trainer-equals-Assessor configurability", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] ASS-125, ASS-126  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun (ASS-125/126)      **Cluster  **C4b Assessment Types      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **Record that in Security the Trainer and Assessor are the same role, so invigilated-session management (ASS-125) and group observations (ASS-126) can sit with the combined Trainer / Assessor role; make the role assignment configurable so Airside can adopt the same pattern.\n\n**Why.  **Reflects HAL's Security operating model and lets other divisions opt in (relates to A32 trainer track and B4 group observation).", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A62": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Consolidated wording / AC clarifications", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-11, CMS-102, CMS-103, CMS-106, CMS-107, CMS-115; ASS-71, ASS-75, ASS-123  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Clarification round, 9 Jun      **Cluster  **various      **Report  **[WR \u00a71]\n\n**Change.  **A set of small wording and AC clarifications agreed in the review: generalise CMS-11 (Review & Publish) so it reads as audience-agnostic rather than operational-only; make explicit on CMS-102/103 the standard the QA assesses against (the configured marking criteria / blueprint); broaden the persona wording on CMS-107 (workforce progression not owned solely by one manager) and exclude FHS from the CMS-106 progression-appetite survey; confirm multi-tier assignment in the CMS-115 acceptance criteria; clarify that assessment scheduling (ASS-71) is an assignable privilege; confirm Learning Delivery Manager visibility of external-assessment reporting is built-in, not a manual hand-off (ASS-75); and make explicit that administrator-managed user / role data is sourced from the Theo feed, with manual creation where a person is not yet in Theo (ASS-123, open question LHR004).\n\n**Why.  **Each is a minor clarification the team raised that nonetheless needs to land in the story text.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A63": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Stage access gate \u2014 within-track sequencing", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-9  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Engineering TNCA Mapping session, 2 Jul 2026      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **\u2014\n\n**Change.  **The existing Section D (Progression Criteria) of CMS-9 addresses the formal progression decision \u2014 minimum time in grade, auto-progression triggers, and manager approval. It does not distinguish this from stage access control: whether the system prevents an individual from seeing or recording evidence against Stage N+1 requirements until Stage N is fully signed off. Add a stage access gate capability to CMS-9\u2019s acceptance criteria: (a) a per-competency, per-stage-pair access gate toggle, off by default, configurable by the Competency Administrator; (b) when a gate is enabled, Stage N+1 requirements are visible to the individual but locked \u2014 read-only and clearly labelled \u2014 until Stage N is fully signed off (all mandatory requirements met and certified); (c) Stage N sign-off automatically unlocks Stage N+1 for that individual, without requiring a separate manual action by a manager; (d) managers and administrators can view locked stages in read-only mode; recording evidence against a locked stage requires an explicit admin override with justification and audit entry; (e) the individual\u2019s profile view shows the unlock condition plainly: \u201cUnlocks when [Stage N name] is complete\u201d.\n\n**Why.  **Engineering\u2019s TNCA structure requires training core to be fully achieved before training proficient can start, and proficient before competency evidence can begin. The existing progression-criteria language covers formal advancement between levels but not whether next-stage requirements are accessible before the gate is passed. The distinction matters at build: a progression gate is a workflow approval step; a stage access gate is a record-access control. Without explicit acceptance criteria, PS may implement progression logic only and leave Stage 2 requirements visible and recordable before Stage 1 is certified, defeating Engineering\u2019s intended sequencing model.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A64": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Assessee attestation \u2014 shared-device mechanism + DNXCT extension", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] ASS-58, ASS-92  \u00b7  [Functional] AS-F A-021, A-022      **Source  **Security Recurrent Training process mapping, 2 Jul 2026      **Cluster  **C4b Assessment Types      **Report  **\u2014\n\n**Change.  **Two extensions to the dual-party attestation model in A35.  (1) Configurable attestation mechanism for shared-device and alternative-login contexts. The existing A35 model \u2014 assessee attests via their own SSO \u2014 only works where the assessee is logged into the device on which the observation form sits. In Security and other shared-device contexts, the observation form runs under the trainer\u2019s login or a generic training account; the assessee has no individual login on that device. Admin must be able to select the attestation mechanism per observation template from three options: (a) Unique identifier \u2014 the assessee enters a value known only to them (e.g., employee number, payroll ID) as a PIN-equivalent confirmation; timestamped and recorded against their record without requiring device login. (b) Stylus signature \u2014 on a device with a touchscreen and stylus, the assessee provides a physical signature captured digitally; equivalent to the wet signature on current paper forms. (c) Email confirmation \u2014 the system sends a confirmation link to the assessee\u2019s registered work email; the assessee opens the link and confirms on their own device, creating an independent timestamp against their account. Whichever mechanism is used, the assessee\u2019s response and the mechanism selected are immutably recorded in the audit trail alongside the observation record.  (2) Extension to third-party result entry. The attestation mechanism applies not only to live practical observations but also to trainer-entered results from third-party systems where no API integration exists. For DNXCT specifically: the trainer reads the result from the CAA website and enters it via an observation-type form in the assessment system, with fields for pass/fail outcome, raw score or result, and an optional evidence upload. The assessee then attests \u2014 using whichever mechanism is configured \u2014 that the entered result matches what they received. This is configured using the standard observation form infrastructure with the assessee attestation toggle enabled, and presented to the assessee as a result-confirmation step rather than a competency observation.\n\n**Why.  **(1) The SSO-based attestation in A35 assumed individual device access. In Security\u2019s recurrent training context, training computers run on generic training accounts; the assessee has no individual login on the device. Without an alternative mechanism the attestation adds no audit value \u2014 the trainer could complete both the observation and the attestation on the same generic account. The configurable mechanism ensures the attestation is genuinely independent of the observer regardless of device setup. The working assumption for Security\u2019s near-term device estate is unique identifier or stylus, pending a device-provision decision by the programme.  (2) DNXCT results are read manually from the CAA website and entered by the trainer; this is the one element of the Security recurrent training process remaining open to human transcription error in the to-be world, because no API integration is achievable. Modelling the result entry as an observation form and applying the assessee attestation removes the need for a separate QA step to verify that the trainer\u2019s entry matches the actual result \u2014 the assessee\u2019s own confirmation, timestamped independently, serves that function. The observation form infrastructure already exists in the system design; this is a configuration choice, not a new build.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A65": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "TIP feed \u2014 14 Jul outcome confirmed (Michael\u2019s judgment-derived output)", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-100  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-047      **Source  **TIP data ownership conversation with Michael Layer / Richard Howlett, 14 July 2026      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **Session Unpack 6 & 14 Jul, Sections 3.5\u20133.10; supersedes part of P-008\n\n**Change.  **Confirm the shape of the TIP data feed following the 14 July conversation with Michael Layer and Richard Howlett. Michael pulls raw scored output from TIPDEMIS/Power BI, but a substantial layer of manual, judgment-based cross-referencing (prior TIP evaluation history, length of service, and the 3-month post-intervention discount period) is then applied before any list is produced. The artefact that should reach the CMS via A47/P-008's import script is therefore Michael's judgment-derived list \u2014 the actual trigger for training bookings \u2014 not the raw TIPDEMIS evaluation output. Confirm also that the outcome value the system consumes is categorical (an assigned category and consequence), not a binary pass/fail, consistent with A47's existing outcome-to-consequence design.\n\n**Why.  **This directly answers the ambiguity flagged at Day 2 Session 3 and carried in P-008 (View A: Michael as administrative conduit, vs View B: Michael applies judgment) in favour of View B, with the nuance that a raw data pull still forms the starting point. It confirms A47's non-binary, outcome-driven design was already correct and needed no structural change \u2014 only this data-provenance clarification, and the residual question of whether Michael's rules can be codified (tracked at the narrowed P-008 below).", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A66": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "TIP logic split: CMS-100 \u2192 new CMS-127", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-100 (amended), CMS-127 (new)  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Source  **Story-alignment review, 14 July 2026      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms (CMS-100); C6 Integrations (CMS-127)      **Report  **Session Unpack 6 & 14 Jul, Section 8.5\n\n**Change.  **Split all TIP outcome-feed, import and provenance logic (A47, A65, and the P-008/P-013 placeholders) out of CMS-100 (Covert Assessment Recording) into a new dedicated story, CMS-127 (TIP Outcome Feed & Import), placed in the Integrations cluster (C6) rather than Reporting & Comms (C5b). CMS-100 is corrected in the same pass: its \"I want to\" and acceptance criteria no longer reference Threat Image Projection, since TIP is confirmed not a covert assessment type (dictionary v1.4, \"TIP score\" entry) \u2014 officers know they are in the system and expect a result within about twenty minutes, unlike a genuine covert test. CMS-100 retains its genuine covert-assessment content (mystery shopper, spot checks, unannounced scenario testing) plus A5 and A30, which remain applicable. P-003 (Security competency decomposition)'s reference to CMS-100 is also removed, as its confirmed home is CMS-2, where it already carries the v0.6 Change Ref.\n\n**Why.  **CMS-100's own comments had already flagged a cluster conflict (register places it in C5b; the TIP feed mechanics are genuinely C6). Left unsplit, the story was accumulating unrelated concerns \u2014 covert-assessment debrief workflow, digital marking, TIP categorisation, and Security competency decomposition \u2014 in a single Comments column, risking PS missing the TIP requirements entirely (searching under \"TIP\" rather than \"Covert Assessment Recording\") or misapplying covert-specific handling (concealment, debrief) to TIP data that explicitly should not have it. Splitting now, while the story is still small, avoids a harder untangling later.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A67": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "DNXCT/TIP equivalency default confirmed non-equivalent", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-118 \u00b7 [Dictionary]      **Source  **Security scheme design walkthrough (Kyla Duigenan), 16 Jul 2026      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **Session Unpack 16 Jul, \u00a74.2; closes OQ-B\n\n**Change.  **Extend CMS-118\u2019s equivalency acceptance criteria (A38) to state explicitly the default behaviour, not only the configured-linkage case: a DNXCT recorded against the standalone recurrent/initial competency window, and a DNXCT recorded as part of a TIP evaluation, are treated as distinct, non-equivalent requirement records by default. Equivalency between the two is applied only where a Competency Administrator has explicitly configured it; absent that configuration, completing one has no effect on the other\u2019s expiry or recurrence window.\n\n**Why.  **A38 as drafted describes the case where equivalency is configured (a DNXCT after a TIP failure can count toward a recurrent, \u201cif configured\u201d) but does not state the default-off position explicitly. Security\u2019s scheme design depends on the default being non-equivalent \u2014 a TIP-linked DNXCT must not silently reset the 13-month recurrent DNXCT clock unless someone has deliberately linked the two. Stating this explicitly in the acceptance criteria removes ambiguity for Publicis Sapient at build and protects the distinction Kyla\u2019s draft scheme manual relies on. Raised as open question OQ-B in the 16 July scheme design walkthrough; this entry closes it.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A68": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "Cross-competency gate extended to ongoing maintenance dependency", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-126 (amended)      **Source  **Security scheme design walkthrough (Kyla Duigenan), 16 Jul 2026      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **Session Unpack 16 Jul, \u00a72.6; closes OQ-A\n\n**Change.  **Extend CMS-126 (B5, Cross-competency Profile Prerequisite Gate) to support a second, distinct dependency mode alongside the existing assignment-time gate, rather than introducing a separate, competing mechanism. Add to CMS-126\u2019s acceptance criteria: (9) when configuring a prerequisite relationship, admin selects a dependency mode \u2014 Assignment gate (existing behaviour, items 1\u20138): checked once, at the point Profile B is assigned; once satisfied, Profile B is unaffected by later changes to Profile A \u2014 or Maintenance dependency (new): checked continuously for as long as Profile B is held, not only at the point of assignment. (10) Under maintenance-dependency mode, if an individual\u2019s status on the prerequisite competency drops below the configured required level after the dependent competency has already been assigned and is in active use (for example: expiry, suspension, restriction, or demotion), the system automatically applies a configurable consequence to the dependent competency for that individual \u2014 alert only, automatic restriction, or automatic suspension \u2014 reusing the consequence-configuration model already defined in CMS-9 (A15). (11) The restriction is automatically lifted for that individual once the prerequisite competency returns to the required level, without requiring separate manual reinstatement \u2014 consistent with the automatic-unlock behaviour in item 3 and in A63. (12) The individual\u2019s profile view shows the reason plainly: \u201cRestricted: [prerequisite competency] fell below [required level] on [date]\u201d, distinguishing this from a restriction caused by the dependent competency\u2019s own recurrent or assessment failure. (13) Notifications trigger to the individual and their line manager both when a maintenance-dependency restriction is applied and when it is lifted. (14) Admin can override a maintenance-dependency-triggered restriction with explicit justification and an approval workflow, recorded in the audit trail, using the same override pattern as item 4 and A63(d). (15) A reporting view lists all individuals currently restricted due to a maintenance dependency, filterable by the dependency pair (for example, \u201cshow everyone restricted on Bag Search due to X-ray\u201d). (16) Maintenance-dependency relationships are versioned with the framework in the same way as assignment gates (item 7); changing a relationship does not retroactively restrict individuals already active unless explicitly reapplied. MoSCoW: items 1\u20138 remain Should Have, conditional on Engineering\u2019s structural decision as originally scoped; items 9\u201316 (the maintenance-dependency mode) are Should Have, unconditional \u2014 Security\u2019s need is independent of Engineering\u2019s architecture question and applies regardless of which structural pattern Engineering adopts.\n\n**Why.  **The 16 July scheme design walkthrough (Kyla Duigenan) surfaced a gap neither CMS-126 nor A63 covers: Security\u2019s bag search competencies (CT lane, 2D enhanced) require the individual to also hold a live X-ray competency; if X-ray is later lost, the dependent bag search competency must be restricted, even though it was validly assigned when X-ray was held. CMS-126 as originally scoped only checks the prerequisite once, at assignment; A63 (CMS-9) only sequences stages within a single competency track \u2014 neither models an ongoing dependency between two already-held, separate competencies. Extending CMS-126 with a second mode reuses the same configuration surface an administrator already uses for the assignment gate, so \u201cdependency between competencies\u201d remains one concept for admins and for PS to build, with the selected mode determining when the check runs and what happens on failure. This also means CMS-126 is no longer purely conditional on Engineering\u2019s architecture decision as B5 originally scoped it \u2014 the maintenance-dependency mode is needed for Security regardless, so CMS-126\u2019s Now/Next positioning should be revisited rather than left at Next by default. Raised as open question OQ-A in the 16 July scheme design walkthrough; this entry closes it.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "A69": {"type": "Amendment", "short": "CSV-import controls for TIP feed (column-mapping, warning, rollback)", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-127 (amended)      **Source  **Jack Andrews catch-up session, 17 July 2026      **Cluster  **C6 Integrations      **Report  **Session Unpack 17 Jul 2026, Section 3.1\n\n**Change.  **Extend CMS-127's import mechanism with three explicit controls, raised as a direct operational-risk concern rather than a design nicety: (1) flexible column-mapping at the point of import \u2014 the admin matches Michael Layer's report columns to system fields at upload time, rather than the story assuming a hard-coded column order that breaks every time his report format changes; (2) a mandatory pre-commit \"tension point\" step that shows a plain-language summary of the action about to be taken (how many records, what outcome, what consequence) before a bulk import is applied, requiring explicit confirmation; (3) a rollback capability that reverses a completed import (and any consequences it triggered, such as restrictions) within a defined window, with the reversal itself recorded in the audit trail.\n\n**Why.  **Jack raised, as a direct operational-risk question rather than a hypothetical, what happens if Michael's monthly import goes wrong \u2014 the concern being that an incorrect bulk import could erroneously place a large number of officers on restriction at once. None of A47, A65, A66 or the P-008 history carried into CMS-127 describe column-mapping flexibility, a pre-commit warning, or rollback; the story currently only specifies that \"the same import script serves both loads,\" which is silent on error containment. This closes that gap before it reaches Publicis Sapient as an ambiguous requirement.\n\n# Part B \u2014 New stories and objects\n\nConfirmed new requirements arising from the workshop. The two scheme-as-system epics (B1, B2) are proposed concretely here; the deeper question of full field-by-field lockstep between the scheme manual and the system is not yet lockable and is held as a placeholder (see P-010).", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "B1": {"type": "New Story", "short": "Scheme governance in-system", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] NEW-E (scheme-as-system, governance)      **Source  **Day 2 Session 1 \u2014 scheme manual      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7B1]\n\n**Change.  **Create an in-system representation of the training-and-competence scheme itself: each division's scheme (Airside, Security, Engineering \u2014 Engineering possibly several, one per business unit) is an object with a named owner, administrator, divisional leads, subject-matter experts and assessment administrator, and a review status (authored by, last reviewed, next review, approved by). The scheme object carries version control in the same way competencies and forms do.\n\n**Why.  **Today the scheme exists only as tacit knowledge plus scattered artifacts; there is no single place that says who owns the model and when it was last assured. Putting the scheme in the system answers the auditor's governance questions and is the spine the export in B2 hangs from.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "B2": {"type": "New Story", "short": "Scheme export at any level", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] NEW-E (scheme-manual export)      **Source  **Day 2 Session 1 \u2014 scheme manual      **Cluster  **C5b Reporting & Comms      **Report  **[WR \u00a7B2]\n\n**Change.  **Once the scheme is populated, the system can export the scheme / framework as a document at any level of the hierarchy \u2014 all of a division, a single business unit, a role, or a single competency down to its requirements and evidence \u2014 ranging from a few pages to hundreds. The export reflects the live, version-controlled content.\n\n**Why.  **Makes the scheme manual a product of the system rather than a separately-maintained document, and lets an auditor or a divisional lead pull exactly the slice they need.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "B3": {"type": "New Story", "short": "Equipment / machinery tag", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] NEW-E (resurrect equipment story)  \u00b7  [Functional] NEW-F      **Source  **Day 2 Session 1      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **[WR \u00a7B3]\n\n**Change.  **Reinstate the equipment-tagging concept: a competency can be tagged as relating to a named piece of machinery or equipment of a given type, so the system can answer \u201cwho is competent on this equipment\u201d and reuse the tag across areas, rather than each area maintaining a separate equipment or vehicle list. This breaks the back of Airside's ~50-vehicle transport spreadsheet and Engineering's asset-specific competencies (for example X-ray machines).\n\n**Why.  **The same equipment appears across divisions and reports; a tag on the competency removes duplicate equipment lists and lets one configurable report serve all of them.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "B4": {"type": "New Story", "short": "Group-observation friction", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] AS-126  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Day 2 Session 3 \u2014 challenge #4      **Cluster  **C4b Assessment Types      **Report  **[WR \u00a7B4]\n\n**Change.  **Add deliberate friction to the group observation so a mis-tick is hard to make: a review step, a submission summary that lists the record about to be created for each individual, and an optional confirmation that requires typing the number of records being created. The group observation remains an accepted-risk workaround for trainers recording several people before a trainer-with-delegate-list interface exists (which arrives with Booking & Scheduling, out of MVP scope).\n\n**Why.  **The operation already carries this risk on its paper end-of-course report; the system can reduce it with prompts rather than removing group observation, which would force one-by-one record look-ups in the interim.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "B5": {"type": "New Story", "short": "Cross-competency profile prerequisite gate", "full": "**Artifact  **[Epic] CMS-126 (new)  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Source  **Engineering TNCA Mapping session, 2 Jul 2026      **Cluster  **C2 Competency Framework Admin      **Report  **\u2014\n\n**Change.  **Create a new epic story (CMS-126, Cross-competency Profile Prerequisite Gate) enabling a Competency Administrator to configure a prerequisite relationship between two separate competency profiles: Profile B cannot be assigned to \u2014 or actively worked towards by \u2014 an individual until they hold Profile A at a specified level. Acceptance criteria: (1) when creating or editing a competency profile, admin can designate one or more prerequisite competencies with a required minimum level (e.g., \u201crequires AGL Proficient at Authorised\u201d); (2) where prerequisites are set, an individual cannot be assigned \u2014 manually or automatically \u2014 to the dependent profile until all prerequisites are satisfied; (3) attempting to assign a profile with unmet prerequisites displays a clear warning listing each unmet prerequisite and its required level; (4) an admin can override the gate with explicit justification and an approval workflow, recorded in the audit trail; (5) the individual\u2019s profile view shows locked aspirational profiles: \u201cUnlocks when [prerequisite] reaches [level]\u201d; (6) when a prerequisite is met, dependent profiles become available for assignment and any applicable notifications trigger; (7) prerequisite relationships are versioned with the framework \u2014 changing a prerequisite on an existing profile does not affect individuals already assigned and actively working on the dependent profile; (8) prerequisite configuration appears in the scheme documentation export (CMS-12, B2). MoSCoW: Should Have \u2014 conditional on whether Engineering resolves to separate profiles per level rather than multi-stage single tracks (in which case A63 suffices).\n\n**Why.  **The Engineering TNCA Mapping session (2 Jul 2026) confirmed that Engineering is weighing two structural approaches: (a) a single competency profile per discipline with stages modelling progression levels (core, proficient, plus), in which case the stage access gate in A63 is sufficient; and (b) separate competency profiles per level, in which case the system must enforce that AGL Plus cannot be assigned until AGL Proficient is held at Authorised status. The between-profile prerequisite is also applicable as the programme matures \u2014 Airside and Security may use specialist tracks that require foundational tracks as prerequisites. Without this story, the separate-profile architecture relies on manual process discipline alone, which the programme explicitly aims to replace.\n\n# Part C \u2014 Placeholders (pending design-stage inputs)\n\nChanges that are directionally clear but not yet lockable. Each is written to stand alone. The triggering action \u2014 who provides the missing input, by when \u2014 is recorded in the companion Workshop Report at the reference shown, and the action register there is what releases each placeholder.", "status": "Closed / Confirmed"}, "P-001": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Restrictions per-type refinement", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-20, CMS-111  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-073\u2013US-082  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Triggering action  **Restrictions table \u2014 [WR Action R2]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Move from a single broad notion of \u201crestriction\u201d to a clear set in which automatic competence-revocation does most of the day-to-day work and a deliberate restriction is reserved for genuine cases (an investigation). Distinguish, and report separately, who is restricted, who is not competent, and who is exempt.\n\n**What is settled.  **Competence equals deployability (per the dictionary). Restrictions are task / duty based, not whole-person. Override remains, auditable, with a mandatory reason. \u201cRevoke\u201d is adopted for competence loss (A48).\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The exact per-type story and dictionary changes cannot be written until the divisions supply an as-is table of every restriction and exemption in use \u2014 what each is for, when it is applied, who applies it, who lifts it, and whether it must reach colleague services / rostering. Security does not use formal reason codes; Airside manages anomalies locally; Engineering largely uses medical restrictions and does not route through HRMS. The table is the evidence base the to-be states are mapped against.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **For each restriction and exemption type, an as-is line and a to-be line stating whether the system applies it automatically (and on what trigger) or a person applies it, and where the notification must go. Expect mostly description and mechanism changes rather than new stories; expect the consolidated dictionary entry for restriction / exemption / suspension / override to be rewritten once the table exists.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-002": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Suspension replacement term", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Dictionary]  \u00b7  [Epic] CMS-20, CMS-111      **Triggering action  **Dictionary v1.4 terminology decision \u2014 [WR Action D1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Name the competency mechanism that temporarily holds an individual's competence pending an intervention (retraining, coaching) with a word that does not collide with the HR meaning of suspension.\n\n**What is settled.  **The mechanism is needed and is distinct from a permanent loss: a temporary hold auto-lifts when its linked activity completes, with no expiry countdown running while held; permanent loss is \u201crevoke\u201d. Engineering is comfortable with \u201csuspension\u201d. The leading candidate is \u201cpause\u201d \u2014 notably, the v0.5 stories already use it.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **In Security and Airside, \u201csuspension\u201d means a disciplinary suspension \u2014 sent home, pending a formal process \u2014 so reusing it for a competency state is confusing and alarming to the front line. The room leaned toward \u201cpause\u201d without formally landing it; \u201cquarantine\u201d and \u201ctemporary hold\u201d remain alternates (\u201cquarantine\u201d is carried as a candidate from an earlier round).\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **A single terminology decision, taken in the dictionary v1.4 design-stage session, that confirms the term (likely \u201cpause\u201d) and applies it consistently across the dictionary entry, the affected stories and any front-line-facing label. Katie is sense-checking the term with Employee Relations, and the dictionary language is being validated with a system architect against Theo / HRMS so the chosen word does not collide with existing system or HR vocabulary. The mechanism itself does not change.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-003": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Security decomposition", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-2, CMS-9 (CMS-100 reference removed by A66 \u2014 not a covert-assessment matter)  \u00b7  [Dictionary]      **Triggering action  **Security scheme worked example \u2014 [WR Action S1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Move Security from \u201cone course broken into modules, with restrictions applied per module\u201d to a hierarchy in which each module is a small competence equal to a duty, so a Security Officer profile is a set of single-module competencies (around 14\u201319), and overall competence is holding the full set.\n\n**What is settled.  **The direction is agreed and is consistent with the rest of the dictionary's model (competence = deployable duty). The driver is the CAA / DfT, which dictates which module is required for which duty.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The exact decomposition cannot be specified until the Security scheme is worked through against its real assessment templates, the CTMD and the end-of-course report \u2014 and the change-management implication (this is a meaningful shift for Security) is understood. The worked example is scheduled for the design stage.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **A Security role-profile register and competency specification expressed as single-module competencies mapped to duties, which will refine the Security stories rather than add many new ones; and a dictionary note that Security competence is now expressed at duty granularity.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-004": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "SSOW / Lauren process", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-15, CMS-24, CMS-113; ASS-61  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **SSOW deep-dive \u2014 [WR Action E1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Replace the manual SSOW tracker and the person-dependent third-party process with a streamlined, single-source-of-truth flow: one external record per person with one unique identifier and a QR credential a control engineer can check, populated automatically for internals and via a single keyed step for externals.\n\n**What is settled.  **Three components are now understood and must be kept distinct: (1) replacing the SSOW tracker via systemisation, which shows the licence outcome; (2) tracking external training completion in lockstep with internal \u2014 confirmed as something HAL already does on spreadsheets, correcting an earlier assumption; and (3) external access, which will not be granted (externals remain shell records behind the firewall, HAL SSO only; no new liability is taken on \u2014 confirmed against ASS-61). The QR credential stays a Must. The clarification round (CMS-15) added that \u201cAuthorised Persons\u201d is better termed \u201cAppointed Persons\u201d for Engineering and must cover skilled and competent persons, not only authorised; and that the SSOW interface, third-party / Trevor-process, card-rolodex and QR stories (CMS-15, CMS-24, CMS-113) should be clustered into one dedicated thread.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The process owner changed: the long-standing \u201cTrevor process\u201d is now the \u201cLauren process\u201d \u2014 Trevor left the business in April, Lauren is still clearing the backlog, and recruitment for the role is in train. This is a live key-person risk, and the exact to-be flow needs a dedicated re-engineering session (DBLX with Sheena, Lauren and Jags) against the real ESR / confined-space examples before the v0.5 stories and wireframes can be confirmed to v0.6. Whether the SSOW record links automatically into the APOC systems or stays a manual step is to be confirmed there.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **A clustered, refined story-and-wireframe set for the external / third-party SSOW flow, expected close to the v0.5 design but adjusted to the operation's preferred flow, with the \u201cAppointed Persons\u201d rename and skilled-and-competent scope, a two-stage input model (internal auto-populated, external single-keyed), one consolidated external identifier replacing the card rolodex, and GDPR retention timestamps. Manual effort is reduced, not eliminated; the residual external-to-HAL manual-input transcription risk (two handover points) is managed by validation in the systemised flow.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-005": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Drawing / tablet question types", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] AS-58, CMS-101  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **Airside question-type list \u2014 [WR Action A2]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Support the question types the operation actually uses in technical papers \u2014 including diagrams and free-hand drawing \u2014 without over-building the assessment engine for MVP.\n\n**What is settled.  **For MVP, a drawing is handled as an evidence upload (a photo or tablet image attached to the assessment), kept separate from the auto-marked questions. A dedicated drawable-area question type, and delivering papers on an iPad under invigilated conditions (a unique start code), are roadmap, not MVP. PS may explore linking the iPad drawing tool as an input type later.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The set of question types to support cannot be finalised until Airside provides the list of types in use (the worked examples cited were an image-of-birds identification question and a free-hand drawing). The list is a design-stage input.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **Confirmation that the assessment engine offers shell types plus evidence-upload for MVP, with an explicit roadmap entry for a drawable-area type and on-tablet invigilated delivery once the question-type list is in.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-006": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "FHS competency definitions", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] ASS-72  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **FHS competency definition \u2014 [WR Action H1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Establish FHS competencies (currently \u201ctrained equals competent\u201d, with no framework) and clarify who owns the FHS competency per role, given FHS courses feed compliance across every division.\n\n**What is settled.  **FHS runs a training-needs analysis of ~45 courses mapped to roles; the same course can map to a different competency in different areas (asbestos is the worked example). The competency layer does not yet exist and the system supports course-then-competency mapping.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The Health & Safety team must define the FHS competencies before they can be modelled. Some FHS competencies are part of Airside core, so this also gates Airside's core scheme work.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **A set of FHS competency definitions and an ownership statement per role, feeding the relevant Airside / Engineering schemes; mostly population and configuration rather than new stories, with a possible ownership-and-governance story if FHS becomes a scheme in its own right.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-007": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Rostering notification interim", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-30, CMS-31, CMS-32  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **Rostering deep-dive \u2014 [WR Action R3]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Define how restriction and exemption changes reach the teams that deploy people, now that the downstream rostering integration (HARRI) is decoupled from Phase 1 and everything stops at the CMS.\n\n**What is settled.  **There is no downstream integration in scope, so the CMS must surface status to whoever deploys \u2014 via dashboard, report and the urgent push of A49. Restrictions are task-based; the late-notification timing gap is the real risk (a late-finishing course can restrict an officer who then starts the next shift before the email is read).\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The interim mechanism needs a working session with one representative from each deploying team (colleague services, scheduling, Maximo) to understand what data they receive today and what they must do with it. Terminal security services already use an Excel tool that blocks deployment against RMS restriction data; Mark Eden is the contact for how that works.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **Likely a small change \u2014 a request queue the deploying team works through, in which every new restriction or exemption is listed for action (estimated around a four-day change) \u2014 and possibly a light feed into the existing Excel tool, pending a future automated rostering suite. Confirmed once the deep-dive establishes what \u201cgood\u201d looks like for the deploying teams.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-008": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "TIP data feed", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-100  \u00b7  [Functional] CMS-F US-047      **Triggering action  **TIP feed conversation \u2014 [WR Action T1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Get TIP results into the CMS routinely so the system applies the configured outcome rule (A47) without the current email-and-Power-BI round trip, and so accountability for acting on results is explicit.\n\n**What is settled.  **The to-be feed is an outcome-driven activity (date range, person, outcome); the system applies the rule rather than computing brackets. The first load is large (historical) and is followed by a monthly delta \u2014 and the same import script serves both, provided the format is consistent.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **Partially resolved \u2014 see A65. The 14 July conversation with Michael Layer and Richard Howlett confirmed the data shape and the ownership question. What remains blocked is the exact codification of Michael's categorisation and returner-logic rules (history look-up, the 3-month discount/holding-pen period, returner category decisioning): whether they are sufficiently formulaic to build into system logic, or must remain a human-judgment step whose output is uploaded to the CMS. A dedicated follow-up working session with Michael Layer is required before this can be finalised (session unpack 14 July, Action A8).\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **A47 stands as designed and needs no structural change: the outcome-driven, non-binary feed and shared import script are confirmed correct. P-008 closes once the follow-up session with Michael Layer documents his rules in enough detail to confirm whether automation is achievable or an upload-of-judgment-output workflow is the permanent shape \u2014 either way, A47's consumption model remains valid.", "status": "Partially resolved"}, "P-009": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Oracle LMS integration", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-4, CMS-9  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **Oracle feasibility calls \u2014 [WR Action L1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Pull e-learning completion data out of Oracle (Theo) into the CMS so an e-learning module can be a requirement within a competency and a person's competence reflects completion automatically. The CMS does not host SCORM \u2014 the content stays in the Oracle estate; only completion data moves.\n\n**What is settled.  **The need and the shape are clear (an API exposing who completed what, when \u2014 TFL runs Oracle this way on a nightly feed). The current manual route (a two-weekly pull, cleansed, then keyed, across three people) leaves the data up to a month behind and creates a compliance gap. The interim is the pass-by-attendance toggle (A42); a nightly SFTP ingest is a fallback to a live API.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **Feasibility hinges on whether Oracle can expose the API and, if so, why HAL has not enabled it. PS hold technical calls in the design phase; the engineering size (e-learning modules \u00d7 audience; the rows moved per cycle) is being quantified. Classroom training is out of integration scope because Booking & Scheduling will replace it.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **If the API exists and can be enabled, this becomes a confirmed C6 integration that may move ahead of the competency-framework cluster; if it cannot, integrations slide down and the pass-by-attendance interim stands. The story set is written so either outcome can be applied.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-010": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Scheme-manual field lockstep", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] (scheme-as-system, B1/B2)  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **Scheme-manual worked examples \u2014 [WR Action SM1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Confirm that every field the scheme manual carries also exists in the system, so the scheme manual can be a faithful product of the system (B2) and the populated content can be script-imported rather than hand-keyed.\n\n**What is settled.  **The scheme-manual structure is agreed (governance and role definition; role-profile register; competency specification with stages, requirements, maintaining-competence, equivalency, failure and consequences, expiry, spot checks, overlay conditions and governance; manual maintenance). The export (B2) and in-system governance (B1) are proposed concretely.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **Field-by-field lockstep can only be proven by working a real example per division through the scheme manual and checking each field against a system field. Those worked examples (Airside first, then Security, then Engineering, with an Engineering deep-dive first) are the design-stage activity that will surface any field with no home.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **A reconciled field list and, where a scheme-manual field has no system home, a targeted story to add it. Until the worked examples are done, the lockstep cannot be asserted; this placeholder converts to confirmed entries as each division's example is completed.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-011": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Bulletin doc auto-replace", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-34, CMS-108  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **Bulletin deep-dive \u2014 [WR Action B1]\n\n**Directional intent.  **Allow a bulletin that publishes a new operating procedure to also replace the superseded document in the shared store, so the read-and-acknowledge flow and the document version stay in step.\n\n**What is settled.  **This is explicitly not MVP \u2014 a future phase. The interim is a manual step: replace the document, then trigger the CMS bulletin. The capability is likely valuable to Health & Safety and Engineering as well as Airside.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The exact behaviour needs a short deep-dive with the Airside owner (Emily) on the current auto-replace use case, scheduled for the design stage as part of PS orientation; the recurrence-as-Expiry reconciliation noted in Part D is adjacent.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **A future-phase story for document auto-replacement linked to bulletin publication, with the MVP interim documented as the manual two-step. Held out of MVP unless re-prioritised.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-012": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "Audit Hub (assurance capability)", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] (new \u2014 to confirm)  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **DBLX covering note (to be shared)\n\n**Directional intent.  **Provide a single assurance / audit view across the competence and assessment estate, so an auditor or regulator can be served from one place rather than from scattered logs \u2014 proposed in the DBLX covering note and surfaced here so it is not lost.\n\n**What is settled.  **The need is real and was reinforced by the clarification round: the audit trail is critical for all regulators, not the CAA alone (see A56). The immutable audit foundation the build already carries is the substrate an Audit Hub would draw on.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **The proposal lives in a DBLX covering note that is not yet in this specification's source set, so the scope, whether it is a new epic or a reporting view over existing stories, and its MoSCoW position cannot be fixed here. It is held as a flagged placeholder pending that note.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **Most likely either a new epic (a dedicated Audit Hub) or a configured assurance view assembled from the audit-trail and reporting stories. To be confirmed once the covering note is provided; until then no story is cut.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-013": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "TIP evaluation-frequency safeguard (6mo warning / 12mo restriction)", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] CMS-4 (A15 recurrence), CMS-100, CMS-111  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **TIP data ownership conversation, 14 July 2026 \u2014 decision pending Rob + Katie (OQ6)\n\n**Directional intent.  **Guard against a colleague indefinitely avoiding TIP evaluation \u2014 by avoiding screening duty and never accumulating the 20-image / 6-month threshold a valid evaluation requires \u2014 by adding an automated warning at the 6-month mark and an automatic restriction / loss of X-ray competence if no valid evaluation is recorded within a rolling 12-month period.\n\n**What is settled.  **Michael Layer confirmed there is currently no automatic safeguard for this scenario \u2014 a colleague could in theory avoid evaluation entirely with no restriction resulting until someone notices. The recurrence engine already includes a consequence-on-non-compliance model (CMS-4 A15: alert only / automatic suspension after grace period / immediate restriction) that is directionally the right mechanism to instantiate this against.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **Whether this is worth building as a new placeholder depends on a prioritisation decision by Rob and Katie (see OQ6, session unpack 14 July). It also depends on resolving an adjacent gap: the v0.5 functional build currently expresses recurrence through the requirement's Expiry field rather than the richer A15 recurrence-with-consequence-chain model (see Part D, Recurrence). Until that reconciliation happens, it is not confirmed that a rolling 12-month \"no valid evaluation\" trigger, distinct from a simple expiry date, can be built as designed.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **If prioritised, a configured recurrence rule against the TIP evaluation requirement: a warning notification at 6 months if evaluation criteria (minimum image count) are unmet, and an automatic restriction / competence-revocation at 12 months if still unmet \u2014 implemented via the same outcome/consequence framework as A47, once the Expiry-vs-recurrence-object reconciliation is settled.\n\n**Provenance note (added 17 July 2026).  **A discrepancy was raised as to whether P-013 was ever associated with a new-starter cohort/class identifier concept. Checked against Change Specification updates 1 through 6: P-013 does not appear at all before update 3, where it was created for the first time and assigned directly to this TIP evaluation-frequency safeguard. The cohort/class identifier idea (see P-014, above) has never held a placeholder number in this specification. The most likely explanation is that an earlier working note anticipated the cohort idea would become \"the next placeholder\" before P-013 was actually allocated elsewhere on 14 July \u2014 not a renumbering or a dropped item.", "status": "Open / Pending"}, "P-014": {"type": "Placeholder", "short": "New-starter position code / cohort identification", "full": "**Artifacts in scope  **[Epic] (candidate \u2014 Theo/CMS profile categorisation)  \u00b7  [Functional]      **Triggering action  **Rob + Katie decision on whether to formalise \u2014 Jack Andrews catch-up session, 17 July 2026\n\n**Directional intent.  **Represent a new starter in Oracle/Theo via a distinct position code (for example, \"Security Officer \u2013 New Starter\"), separate from the substantive position code they will hold once signed off, so the CMS can identify a cohort of new starters by name and start date without requiring full Booking & Scheduling functionality.\n\n**What is settled.  **Nothing formally \u2014 this was raised in conversation by Jack Andrews, not agreed. The existing level structure (New Starter Training \u2192 Proficient) already carries a version of this distinction at the competency layer, which may reduce or remove the need for a separate Theo-side position code; the two are not yet reconciled.\n\n**What is blocked, and why.  **Whether this is worth pursuing at all depends on Rob and Katie deciding whether the existing level-based distinction is sufficient, or whether a genuine Theo-side position code is needed \u2014 and if so, whether that is an Oracle/Theo change (outside CMS scope) or a CMS-side categorisation only. This has not been scoped against the \"light\" Booking & Scheduling scope question (see Part F) with which it directly overlaps.\n\n**Likely shape of the v0.6 change.  **If pursued and if a genuine Theo-side change is ruled out, most likely a CMS-side profile attribute or tag identifying new-starter status and start date, feeding the same cohort-identification need discussed in Part F, rather than a new epic in its own right. To be confirmed once Rob and Katie have decided whether to progress it.\n\n# Part D \u2014 Dictionary changes (v1.3 \u2192 v1.4)\n\nConsolidated changes to the competency dictionary. The current v1.3 already carries several earlier corrections \u2014 TNCA is defined correctly (Training Needs Competency Analysis, not a procurement term), TIP is expanded as Threat Image Projection (correcting the earlier \u201cTechnical Interception Proficiency\u201d), and the manipulation-overlay terms are flagged as under review. The changes below are the new ones arising from the workshop. Several also appear as artifact-tagged entries in Parts A and C; they are gathered here so the v1.4 edit can be made in one pass. The divisional description reviews now under way \u2014 SSOW, TNCA and Spot Check (Adam and Sheena) and TIP and Covert (Steph and Kyla) \u2014 validate the wording of these entries directly (Workshop Report Actions D2 and D3).\n\n| **Term** | **Type** | **Change for v1.4** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Covert assessment | Amend | Correct the definition to reflect Security practice: covert testing is conducted by a third party (Redline), which places a threat item through the X-ray or body scanner; the result is recorded by the third party / Security manager, not by a Security assessor, and trainers are not permitted to conduct it. Remove the statement that TIP tests are the primary covert assessment type (see TIP, below). |\n| TIP score | Amend | Decouple TIP from covert assessment: TIP is not covert \u2014 officers know they are in the system and expect to see a projected image within around twenty minutes. Recharacterise TIP as a live-lane performance metric rather than a test conducted in a controlled environment. Retain the Threat Image Projection expansion and the link to automatic competency outcomes (A47). |\n| Spot check | Amend | Note that Security does not offer spot checks; Engineering and Airside would. Rework the illustrative example so it does not imply a Security spot check, and present it as a configurable recurring activity (for example, a set number per year recorded as an activity). |\n| Toolbox Talk | Add / amend | Define Toolbox Talk as a formal short safety briefing recorded against competence to maintain, capturing deliverer and attendees \u2014 explicitly not informal training. Note the term is retained across all divisions (A27). |\n| Restriction / Exemption / Suspension / Override | Amend (pending P-001/P-002) | Once the restrictions table exists, rewrite this entry so it distinguishes automatic competence-revocation from deliberate restriction, records that restrictions are task-based, adopts \u201crevoke\u201d for permanent competence loss, and replaces \u201csuspension\u201d with the agreed term \u201cpause\u201d, already used in the stories. Capture that \u201csuspension\u201d carries a disciplinary meaning in Security and Airside. |\n| Observation form / Checklist | Amend | Add the configurable dual-party model option \u2014 assessor completion plus a separate, prerequisite-gated assessee attestation timestamped to the assessee's login (A35) \u2014 and the configurable result format (pass/fail, level scale, or percentage), including Airside's 0\u20135 scale (A37). Confirm forms are versioned and the in-force version is recorded (A36). |\n| Bulletin | Amend | Hold the language to acknowledge (not accept); note a bulletin can carry the Toolbox Talk read-and-understand function and chain to a confidence self-assessment (A28, A29). |\n| Recurrence | Reconcile | Carry the existing implementation note: in the v0.5 functional build recurrence is expressed through the requirement's Expiry field rather than as a distinct recurrence object with consequence chains. The richer model (CMS-4 A15) is flagged for reconciliation; the workshop confirmed recurrent is modelled as a stage of time-windowed requirements, and that a requirement may be persistent across competencies. |\n| Equivalency | Amend | Add the worked patterns: an in-life observation is equivalent to refresher training (Airside firearms), and an assessment event done for one reason can satisfy a separate window if configured (DNXCT after a TIP failure counting toward a recurrent) \u2014 A38. |\n| Audit trail / activity log | Amend | Generalise the language so it is regulator-agnostic: the immutable audit and activity record is critical for all audits and all regulators (CAA, DfT and others), not Security / CAA alone (A56). Carried across the affected stories (CMS-90; ASS-108/110/122/124). |\n| Appointed Persons[^c17] | Add / amend | For Engineering, \u201cAuthorised Persons\u201d is better expressed as \u201cAppointed Persons\u201d, and the term must cover skilled and competent persons, not only authorised (A55-adjacent; CMS-15). Define it and align the SSOW / third-party stories to it (P-004). |\n| Audit Hub | Add (proposed) | A proposed consolidated assurance / audit capability (see placeholder P-012). Recorded as a term pending the DBLX covering note that defines its scope; not yet a confirmed object. |\n| New AS-IS terms (glossary) | Add | Add the operational system and acronym terms PS need to orient (see Part E): APOC / Control Engineer, CTMD (Compliance Tracking Monthly Data), DNXCT / DNX, Redline, ESR (Electrical Safety Regulations), SIN / CIM (Certified Instructor Number), AirDat / ACMF / AirVA, ILP, Maximo, HRMS / RMS, the Lauren process (formerly Trevor process), AOA / AOL / SAO, GSO / GSS / ASM, the equipment / machinery tag, and \u201crevoke\u201d. |\n\n# Part E \u2014 Summary cross-reference\n\nEvery entry in this specification, the artifact it lands in, its v0.6 change status, and the companion Workshop Report action that carries it (where applicable). This is the working index for the v0.6 cut.\n\n| **Ref** | **Change** | **Artifact** | **v0.6 status** | **Report** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| A25 | Mirror at stage/level (confirmed in wireframe) | Epic / Functional | No change (confirm) | [WR \u00a7A25] |\n| A26 | Scheduled / pre-published status | Dictionary | Dictionary add (stories support) | [WR \u00a7A26] |\n| A27 | Toolbox Talk formal | Epic / Dictionary | Amended | [WR \u00a7A27] |\n| A28 | Bulletin acknowledge-not-accept | Epic / Dictionary | Amended | [WR \u00a7A28] |\n| A29 | Confidence self-assessment | Epic / Functional | New | [WR \u00a7A29] |\n| A30 | Digital assessment, no transcription | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A30] |\n| A31 | ASM certificate production | Epic | Amended | [WR \u00a7A31] |\n| A32 | Trainer track / SIN | Epic | Amended | [WR \u00a7A32] |\n| A33 | End-of-course report \u2192 system | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A33] |\n| A34 | Two observation types / coaching lifts | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A34] |\n| A35 | Dual-party observation form | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A35] |\n| A36 | Form version tracking | Epic / Dictionary | Amended | [WR \u00a7A36] |\n| A37 | Configurable result format / 0\u20135 scale | Epic / Dictionary | Amended | [WR \u00a7A37] |\n| A38 | Equivalency patterns | Epic / Dictionary | Amended | [WR \u00a7A38] |\n| A39 | Configurable report builder | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A39] |\n| A40 | Widgetised dashboards | Epic | Amended | [WR \u00a7A40] |\n| A41 | Rich-text descriptions | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A41] |\n| A42 | Pass-by-attendance toggle | Epic / Functional | New | [WR \u00a7A42] |\n| A43 | Role-tag track assignment | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A43] |\n| A44 | Self-registration dual-track | Functional | No change (confirm) | [WR \u00a7A44] |\n| A45 | Offline exam fallback / timed | Epic / Functional | No change (confirm) | [WR \u00a7A45] |\n| A46 | Absence / pause / RTD | Epic / Functional / Dictionary | No change (confirm) | [WR \u00a7A46] |\n| A47 | TIP outcome-driven feed | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A47] |\n| A48 | Restriction refined / revoke | Epic / Functional / Dictionary | Amended | [WR \u00a7A48], P-001 |\n| A49 | Urgent restriction push | Epic / Functional | Amended | [WR \u00a7A49], P-007 |\n| A50 | Notification recipient-routing | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | CMS-30/31/32 |\n| A51 | Configurable results release to assessee | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | CMS-35 |\n| A52 | Certificate download control + exceptions | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | CMS-36 |\n| A53 | Dashboard shows restrictions + route-to-clear | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | CMS-45 |\n| A54 | Offline mode repositioned + invigilation-gated | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | CMS-59 |\n| A55 | TNCA create/display/track lifecycle | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | [WR SM1] |\n| A56 | Audit generalised beyond CAA | Epic / Functional / Dictionary | Amended (clarification) | CMS-90 |\n| A57 | Configurable retake routing | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | ASS-29/63 |\n| A58 | Technical-readiness check repositioned | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | ASS-38 |\n| A59 | Assessment Administrator role defined | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | ASS-69 |\n| A60 | Manual external entry stores scanned certs | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | ASS-72/73 |\n| A61 | Trainer-equals-Assessor configurability | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | ASS-125/126 |\n| A62 | Consolidated wording / AC clarifications | Epic / Functional | Amended (clarification) | 9 Jun review |\n| B1 | Scheme governance in-system | Epic | New | [WR \u00a7B1] |\n| B2 | Scheme export at any level | Epic | New | [WR \u00a7B2] |\n| B3 | Equipment / machinery tag | Epic / Functional | New | [WR \u00a7B3] |\n| B4 | Group-observation friction | Epic / Functional | New | [WR \u00a7B4] |\n| A63 | Stage access gate \u2014 within-track sequencing | Epic / Functional | Amended | Engineering TNCA session, 2 Jul 2026 |\n| B5 | Cross-competency profile prerequisite gate | Epic | New | Engineering TNCA session, 2 Jul 2026 |\n| A64 | Assessee attestation \u2014 shared-device mechanism + DNXCT extension | Epic / Functional | Amended | Security recurrent mapping, 2 Jul 2026 |\n| P-001 | Restrictions per-type refinement | Epic / Functional / Dictionary | Placeholder-pending | [WR R2] |\n| P-002 | Suspension replacement term | Dictionary / Epic | Placeholder-pending | [WR D1] |\n| P-003 | Security decomposition | Epic / Dictionary | Placeholder-pending | [WR S1] |\n| P-004 | SSOW / Lauren process | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending | [WR E1] |\n| P-005 | Drawing / tablet question types | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending | [WR A2] |\n| P-006 | FHS competency definitions | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending | [WR H1] |\n| P-007 | Rostering notification interim | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending | [WR R3] |\n| P-008 | TIP data feed | Epic / Functional | Partially resolved \u2014 see A65 | [WR T1] |\n| A65 | TIP feed \u2014 14 Jul outcome confirmed (Michael\u2019s judgment-derived output) | Epic / Functional | Amended (confirmatory) | Session Unpack 6&14 Jul \u00a7\u00a73.5\u20133.10 |\n| P-013 | TIP evaluation-frequency safeguard (6mo warning / 12mo restriction) | Epic (candidate) | Placeholder \u2014 pending Rob+Katie | Session Unpack 14 Jul, OQ6 |\n| A66 | TIP logic split: CMS-100 \u2192 new CMS-127 | Epic / Dictionary | Amended + New | Session Unpack 14 Jul, \u00a78.5 |\n| A67 | DNXCT/TIP equivalency default confirmed non-equivalent | Epic / Dictionary | Amended | Session Unpack 16 Jul; closes OQ-B |\n| A68 | Cross-competency gate extended to ongoing maintenance dependency | Epic (CMS-126, amended) | Amended | Session Unpack 16 Jul; closes OQ-A |\n| P-009 | Oracle LMS integration | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending | [WR L1] |\n| P-010 | Scheme-manual field lockstep | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending | [WR SM1] |\n| P-011 | Bulletin doc auto-replace | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending (future) | [WR B1] |\n| P-012 | Audit Hub (assurance capability) | Epic / Functional | Placeholder-pending | DBLX covering note |\n| A69 | CSV-import controls for TIP feed (column-mapping, warning, rollback) | Epic | Amended | Session Unpack 17 Jul 2026, \u00a73.1 |\n| P-014 | New-starter position code / cohort identification | Epic (candidate) | Placeholder \u2014 pending Rob+Katie | Session Unpack 17 Jul 2026, \u00a73.2 |\n\n# Part F \u2014 Scope and MoSCoW notes\n\nStatus notes that bear on what is built, and when, but are not themselves story edits.\n\n- **Booking ****&**** Scheduling stays out of MVP** but is reinforced as a funded block: it carries the trainer-with-delegate-list interface, course registration, attendance and the classroom concept the group observation works around, and would remove a large administrative load. Several interim positions in this document exist only because it is deferred.\n\n- **Classroom training is out of integration scope** \u2014 Booking & Scheduling will replace it, so the Oracle integration (P-009) targets e-learning completion only.\n\n- **Integration sequencing is conditional** \u2014 if the Oracle API can be enabled (P-009), the integrations cluster (C6) may move ahead of the competency-framework cluster (C2), because selecting an LMS module as evidence then needs the integration in place; if not, integrations slide down (there is no HARRI, only Oracle competence in/out).\n\n- **HARRI is decoupled from Phase 1** \u2014 everything stops at the CMS, which is why the rostering-notification interim (P-007) and the urgent-push behaviour (A49) matter now.\n\n- **The SSOW QR credential stays a Must** (P-004) \u2014 control engineers must be able to check a credential in the field even though the wider SSOW build is light.\n\n- **Group observation is an accepted risk** (B4) \u2014 retained as a Should with friction controls, on the basis that the operation already carries the equivalent risk on paper.\n\n**\u201cLight\u201d Booking ****&**** Scheduling cohort/class acceleration** \u2014 raised 17 July 2026 (Jack Andrews catch-up session) as an open scope question: whether to accelerate a limited cohort/class management capability into MVP, funded incrementally, to remove the residual manual Excel tracking new-starter cohorts currently depend on. Distinct from P-007 (notification interim only). Unresolved \u2014 pending a scope and funding decision by Rob and Katie; not yet a story edit.\n\n# Part G \u2014 Notes for the v0.6 cut\n\n### v0.6 workbook traceability columns\n\nAdd two columns to the v0.6 workbooks, mirroring the existing v0.5 \u201cChange Status\u201d pattern (and the replaced/amended duplicate-row convention) in column M:\n\n- **v0.6 Change Status** \u2014 one of: New, Amended, Replaced, No change, Placeholder-pending.\n\n- **v0.6 Change Ref** \u2014 the entry reference in this specification (for example A35, B3, P-004).\n\nSpecify the columns now; populate them when the v0.6 cut is made. Where a story is replaced or materially amended, follow the existing duplicate-row pattern rather than overwriting the v0.5 line.\n\n| **One amendment ledger, two generations.  **The workbook's Comments already carry the v0.5 amendment codes A1\u2013A24. This specification continues that single ledger: the 0.5\u21920.6 amendments are numbered from A25 onward (workshop-derived A25\u2013A49; clarification-round-derived A50\u2013A62), so a code never means two different things. New stories use the B-series and design-stage placeholders the P-series \u2014 both unused in v0.5, so they do not clash. The \u201cv0.6 Change Ref\u201d column therefore sits cleanly alongside the v0.5 codes already in the comments. |\n| --- |\n\n### Build-cluster column\n\nAdd a build-cluster column populated from the sprint-sequencing audit register \u2014 clusters only (C0\u2013C6 and the deferred groupings), not sprint numbers, since sprint allocation is PS's to set. New and placeholder stories carry TBC until the design phase places them. The cluster shown against each entry above is the indicative starting point for that column.\n\n### Recommended additional artifact \u2014 AS-IS systems and acronyms glossary\n\nBeyond the stories and the dictionary, the one artifact worth adding before PS build is a short AS-IS systems and acronyms glossary, so a developer new to the airport can read the requirement set without decoding operational shorthand. It can live as a new section in the dictionary (Part D adds the terms) or as a standalone page. The terms to define are listed in Part D under \u201cNew AS-IS terms\u201d.\n\n| **One line to carry forward.  **Nothing in this specification has been applied to the workbooks yet. It is the agreed list of what to change; the v0.6 cut and the v1.4 dictionary edit are the next working session, and the placeholders are released by the actions in the companion Workshop Report. |\n| --- |\n\n\tDBLX  \u00b7  679806  \u00b7  Story & Wireframe Change Specification v0.5 \u2192 v0.6 \u2014 Draft for design-stage alignment\tPage 1\n\n## Comments\n\n[^c17] Robert Ashcroft: Checking with Katie as I think we have both Authorised AND Appointed and therefore I\u2019m not sure which is which and suggesting both need adding to the dictionary and then the stories updating accordingly.", "status": "Open / Pending"}};
